@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static homepage served with `pnpm start`.
+
+## Passkeys in "My Account"
+
+The **My Account** link (or `/#account`) opens a dialog where customers can:
+
+- **Create an account** with just a name, email, and a passkey (no password).
+- **Sign in** with a passkey — from the email field's autofill (conditional UI) or the "Sign in with a passkey" button.
+- **Manage saved passkeys** — see where each is stored, when it was added and last used, whether it is synced, and rename, add, or remove them (the last passkey can't be removed).
+- **Update their profile** — name changes save directly; email changes require reauthentication.
+- **Reauthenticate** with a user-verified passkey before adding/removing passkeys, changing email, or deleting the account (sign-ins older than 5 minutes).
+
+Passkey providers are kept in sync through the WebAuthn Signal API where the browser supports it.
+
+| File | Role |
+| --- | --- |
+| `js/account.js` | Dialog UI and flows |
+| `js/passkey-client.js` | `navigator.credentials` calls, JSON serialization, error copy, Signal API |
+| `js/relying-party.js` | Relying party: challenges, verification, accounts, sessions |
+| `js/base64url.js` | Encoding helpers |
+
+Because this site is static, `js/relying-party.js` runs in the browser and stores accounts in
+`localStorage`. It still verifies everything (single-use challenges, origin, RP ID hash, UP/UV flags,
+ES256/RS256 signatures, sign counters), but data that lives in the browser can't be trusted.
+Before real launch, move it behind a backend — every method takes and returns JSON and lines up with
+the REST endpoints listed at the top of the file, so `account.js` only needs its `api` calls switched to `fetch`.
+
+Passkeys require HTTPS (or `localhost`).
